  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThe combination of a weak mid level short wave and deep moisture \n(PWATs 2.0-2.2 inches) will result in a more active sea breeze \ntoday. The best chance (30-40%) will be along the coastline up to \nthe Victoria Crossroads. Low to very low rain chances are expected \nfor Thursday as the high pressure aloft regains it's hold over S TX \nand maintains the well above normal temperatures.\n\nA cold front Friday will bring some relief from the heat with highs \nthis weekend around 5-10 degrees cooler and lows around 10-15 \ndegrees cooler. The temperatures begin to warm back up early next \nweek.\n\nAhead of the cold front on Friday, rain chances increase to a 20-30% \nwith up to 40% along the coastline. The highest chance up to 80% \nwill be over the gulf waters where the deepest moisture will reside. \nAt this time, it looks like mainly showers with a few thunderstorms \nremaining sub-severe.\n\nWinds are progged to shift to the northeast Friday morning ahead of \nthe main cold front, then strengthen throughout the day as high \npressure builds across the region leading to a breezy to windy day. \nThe cooler and drier airmass is forecast to arrive later in the day \non Friday and into the overnight hours. This weekend looks to be \npleasant with winds relaxing along with a cooler and drier airmass.\n\nAs for minor coastal flooding, will let the Coastal Flood Advisory \nexpire at 1 AM based on lower observed tide levels and cameras along \nthe islands showing that the water is not quite up to the dunes. \nStrong northeast winds across the gulf waters in the wake of the \ncold front Friday through Saturday will likely lead to minor coastal \nflooding once again. Seas are forecast to build to 10-12 feet and \nswell periods increase to 8-9 seconds. All of which are conducive \nfor pushing water up to the dunes during times of high tide. If the \nwinds and seas are slightly higher than expected, a Coastal Flood \nWarning may be warranted with water up to 3 feet above MSL during \nhigh tide. However, confidence is very low that we will see tides \nthat high. Will continue to monitor trends as we go through the week.",
